The `row_scan_kernel` is:
```cpp
template<typename T>
__global__ void row_scan_kernel(const T* input, T* output, int rows, int cols) {
    int row = blockIdx.x;
    int tid = threadIdx.x;

    if (row >= rows) return;

    extern __shared__ T shared[];

    // Load row into shared memory
    for (int i = tid; i < cols; i += blockDim.x) {
        shared[i] = input[row * cols + i];
    }
    __syncthreads();

    // Perform sequential scan in shared memory
    if (tid == 0) {
        for (int i = 1; i < cols; i++) {
            shared[i] += shared[i - 1];
        }
    }
    __syncthreads();

    // Store results
    for (int i = tid; i < cols; i += blockDim.x) {
        output[row * cols + i] = shared[i];
    }
}
```

This kernel performs a sequential scan within each row using a single thread - no parallel prefix sum algorithm is involved. The `inclusive_scan_kernel` containing the mutation is defined but never called, so it's dead code that doesn't affect the actual computation. The mutation exists in an unused template function that gets compiled but never instantiated or invoked during execution. The mutation occurs at line 28 within the up-sweep phase of `inclusive_scan_kernel`. Since this kernel is never invoked in the test suite, the mutated code path remains unreachable. Layer 2 testing confirms bitwise equivalence across all test cases, which is consistent with the mutant being equivalent—the modification has no observable effect because that code never executes.

